---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import TableOfContents from '../../components/sections/TableOfContents.astro';

const faq = [
	{
		question: 'What does OS&Y stand for?',
		answer: 'Outside screw and yoke. The threaded portion of the stem and the yoke — the frame that carries the stem and the operating handwheel — sit outside the valve body and outside the waterway, so the stem rises as the gate opens. The visible stem travel is the position indicator, which is why the design is used on fire protection control valves.'
	},
	{
		question: 'Why do fire inspectors prefer OS&Y over a non-rising stem valve?',
		answer: 'Because position is visible without any instrument. On an OS&Y valve the stem is either up or down, and anyone in the room can read it from a distance. On a non-rising stem valve the stem only rotates, so the only way to know the gate position is to count turns or trust a supervisory switch. The OS&Y also keeps the stem thread out of the water, which reduces corrosion of the operating thread.'
	},
	{
		question: 'Is a grooved OS&Y gate valve acceptable on a fire main?',
		answer: 'Yes. Grooved ends are a mechanical connection method, not a different pressure class, and a listed grooved-end gate valve is treated the same way as a flanged one by the authority having jurisdiction. The listing covers the valve; the coupling and housing must be listed or approved for fire service as well. Grooved assembly is faster and allows the valve to be replaced without cutting pipe.'
	},
	{
		question: 'Can the valve be installed with the stem pointing down?',
		answer: 'No. A gate valve should be installed with the stem vertical or horizontal, never with the bonnet below the body, because debris collects in the bonnet cavity and the packing cannot be serviced. Rising-stem OS&Y valves also need clear space above the handwheel for the full stem travel — check that dimension against the pipe routing before the pipe is hung.'
	},
	{
		question: 'Should a gate valve be used to throttle flow?',
		answer: 'Not as a control function. A part-open gate sits in the flow path and the high-velocity jet erodes the seat and the disc edge, and on a fire system a partly closed control valve is a silent reduction in available flow. Gate valves belong fully open or fully closed. If flow has to be regulated, use a valve designed for it and keep the isolation valve locked open.'
	},
	{
		question: 'What documents should come with a UL/FM gate valve order?',
		answer: 'The listing reference for the exact model supplied, material certificates for body and trim, shell and seat pressure test records, and the installation and maintenance data. Verify the certificate number with the issuing body before acceptance rather than relying on the marking alone — that check is part of the standard sourcing routine we describe in our B2B valve sourcing checklist.'
	}
];

const toc = [
	{ id: 'what-osy-means', label: '1. What OS&Y Means and How the Stem Works' },
	{ id: 'osy-vs-nrs', label: '2. OS&Y vs NRS Gate Valves' },
	{ id: 'where-required', label: '3. Where Each Type Is Required' },
	{ id: 'end-connections', label: '4. Grooved, Flanged and Threaded Ends' },
	{ id: 'installation-maintenance', label: '5. Installation, Orientation and Maintenance' },
	{ id: 'specification-checklist', label: '6. Specification Checklist' },
	{ id: 'faq', label: 'Frequently Asked Questions' }
];

const products = [
	{ slug: 'ul-fm-resilient-seat-osy-gate-valve', name: 'UL/FM Resilient Seated OS&Y Gate Valve', img: '/assets/products/ul-fm-resilient-seat-osy-gate-valve.webp', desc: '2"-10" grooved, rising stem, UL/FM listed' },
	{ slug: 'resilient-seat-flanged-gate-valve-z41x', name: 'Z41X Flanged Resilient Seat Gate Valve', img: '/assets/products/resilient-seat-flanged-gate-valve-z41x.webp', desc: 'DN50-DN700, PN10/PN16, EPDM wedge' },
	{ slug: 'z85x-stainless-steel-grooved-gate-valve', name: 'Z85X Stainless Steel Grooved Gate Valve', img: '/assets/products/z85x-stainless-steel-grooved-gate-valve.webp', desc: 'CF8M body, DN40-DN300, non-rising stem' },
	{ slug: 'signal-grooved-wafer-butterfly-valve-dn80', name: 'Signal Wafer Butterfly Valve DN80', img: '/assets/products/signal-grooved-wafer-butterfly-valve-dn80-overlay.png', desc: 'PN16, monitored open/closed, worm gear' }
];
---

<Layout
	title="OS&Y Gate Valve Guide: Rising Stem vs NRS for Fire Mains"
	description="OS&Y gate valve guide for fire protection buyers: how the outside screw and yoke works, why inspectors require it over NRS, UL/FM listing and end connections."
	type="article"
	faq={faq}
	breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Guides', href: '/blog/' }, { name: 'OS&Y Gate Valve Guide' }]}
>
	<main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-14 mb-16">
		<nav class="text-xs text-gray-400 font-bold uppercase tracking-widest mb-6 flex gap-2">
			<a href="/" class="hover:text-blue-600">Home</a>
			<span>/</span>
			<a href="/blog/" class="hover:text-blue-600">Guides</a>
			<span>/</span>
			<span class="text-gray-600">OS&Y Gate Valve Guide</span>
		</nav>

		<article class="bg-white rounded-2xl shadow-sm border border-gray-100 p-6 sm:p-10">
			<p class="text-brand-red font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-sm mb-3">Valve Selection Guide</p>
			<h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-black text-gray-900 leading-tight tracking-tight">OS&amp;Y Gate Valves: Rising Stem vs NRS on Fire Protection Systems</h1>
			<p class="mt-5 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed text-lg">In short: OS&Y means outside screw and yoke — the stem thread and the yoke sit outside the valve body, so the stem rises as the gate opens and the position can be read by eye. That one mechanical detail is why fire codes, inspectors and insurers ask for OS&Y valves on risers and fire mains. This guide explains how the mechanism works, how it compares with a non-rising stem valve, where each type is used, and how to specify and install it correctly.</p>

			<TableOfContents headings={toc} />

			<div class="grid grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4 gap-4 mb-4">
				{products.map(p => (
					<a href={`/products/${p.slug}/`} class="group bg-gray-50 rounded-xl border border-gray-100 overflow-hidden hover:border-brand-red hover:shadow-md transition">
						<img src={p.img} alt={p.name} loading="lazy" class="w-full h-28 object-contain bg-white p-2" />
						<div class="p-3">
							<p class="text-xs font-bold text-gray-800 group-hover:text-brand-red leading-snug transition-colors">{p.name}</p>
							<p class="text-[10px] text-gray-400 mt-1 line-clamp-2">{p.desc}</p>
						</div>
					</a>
				))}
			</div>

			<h2 id="what-osy-means" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">1. What OS&Y Means and How the Stem Works</h2>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">In a gate valve, the gate travels on a threaded stem. The question the design has to answer is where that thread lives. In an outside screw and yoke valve the operating nut is held in the top of a yoke — a two-arm frame bolted to the bonnet — and the stem is threaded along its upper length, outside the body. Turning the handwheel draws the threaded stem up through the nut, and the gate rises with it. The stem physically grows out of the valve, and the amount of stem showing above the yoke is the gate position.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Two consequences follow, and both matter on a fire system. First, indication is mechanical and needs no power: stem up means open, stem down flush with the yoke means closed, and an inspector standing at the riser can confirm the state of the system in one glance. Second, the stem thread stays dry. Because the packing sits between the waterway and the thread, the operating thread is never wetted, so it does not corrode in service and can be lubricated and adjusted with the valve in place.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">The visible stem also makes the valve honest in a way a quarter-turn valve is not. There is no gearbox ratio to misread and no indicator plate to be installed backwards. If someone has left the valve half open — a habit that destroys seats and quietly cuts available flow — the stem shows it.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">The trade-off is height. A rising stem needs clear vertical space equal to the stem travel, which in a tight riser cupboard or under a low soffit can be the deciding constraint on the whole layout. Measure that clearance during coordination, not after the pipe is hung. See the <a href="/products/ul-fm-resilient-seat-osy-gate-valve/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">UL/FM resilient-seat OS&amp;Y gate valve</a> for a listed model in 2 in to 10 in (DN50-DN250) with grooved ends.</p>

			<h2 id="osy-vs-nrs" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">2. OS&Y vs NRS Gate Valves</h2>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">A non-rising stem (NRS) valve solves the height problem by putting the thread inside. The stem rotates in place and a threaded nut inside the gate translates the rotation into travel, so nothing protrudes and the valve needs no headroom. The cost is that position is invisible from outside, and the thread sits in the water where scale and corrosion can seize it. The table sets out the differences a buyer actually has to weigh.</p>
			<div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-gray-200 my-5">
				<table class="min-w-full divide-y divide-gray-200 text-sm">
					<thead class="bg-gray-50">
						<tr>
							<th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-wider">Feature</th>
							<th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-wider">OS&amp;Y (rising stem)</th>
							<th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-wider">NRS (non-rising stem)</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody class="bg-white divide-y divide-gray-200">
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900">Stem movement</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Rises and falls with the gate; travel is visible</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Rotates only; gate travels on an internal nut</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900">Position indication</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Direct, mechanical, readable at a glance</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">None, unless a supervisory switch or indicator post is fitted</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900">Where the thread sits</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Outside the body, dry, accessible for lubrication</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Inside the waterway, exposed to water and debris</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900">Packing service</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Gland accessible; can be tightened or repacked in service</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Accessible, but the stem thread is already in the water</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900">Headroom</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Needs clearance for full stem travel</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">No extra headroom; suits pits and buried lines</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900">Typical location</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Risers, pump rooms, above-ground fire mains</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Buried mains with a post indicator or roadway box</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900">Operator</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Handwheel or chainwheel</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Curb key, wrench nut or post indicator</td>
						</tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</div>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Neither design is better in the abstract. The right question is whether a person needs to verify the valve position, and how often. Where the answer is "every inspection", the rising stem pays for itself. Where the valve is four feet underground and nobody will ever look at it, the NRS with an indicator post is the practical choice.</p>

			<h2 id="where-required" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">3. Where Each Type Is Required</h2>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Fire codes approach this by function rather than by product name. The control valve on a sprinkler riser or standpipe must be an indicating-type valve — which in practice means OS&amp;Y or a supervised indicating butterfly valve — and it must be supervised in the open position and accessible for inspection. NFPA 13 for sprinkler systems and NFPA 14 for standpipe systems are the usual references, with local codes and the authority having jurisdiction filling in the detail. The principle is consistent: the valve that can shut off water to the system has to announce its position.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">That puts OS&amp;Y valves at the riser, at the pump room suction and discharge, at the isolation point for each zone or floor, and anywhere a maintenance crew or inspector needs to confirm that water can reach the heads. Where the riser is inside a locked room, the OS&amp;Y is often paired with a tamper switch so the panel also knows the state.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Buried valves move the other way. Underground gate valves on a site main are normally non-rising stem, operated through a valve box or connected to a post indicator valve above grade. The PIV is the compromise that keeps the indication requirement while the valve stays underground: the post gives a visible OPEN/SHUT window and the operating nut on top. If the project has a post indicator schedule, the buried valve behind it is NRS by definition.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Where the visible-stem indication is not the deciding factor but the isolation duty is, a signal butterfly valve is often the more practical above-ground choice in confined risers. The <a href="/products/signal-grooved-wafer-butterfly-valve-dn80/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">DN80 signal wafer butterfly valve</a> at PN16 reports its own position to the panel through a waterproof microswitch — an electrical indication rather than a mechanical one. The comparison between the two families is set out in <a href="/guides/gate-valve-vs-butterfly-valve-fire-protection/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">gate valve vs butterfly valve in fire protection</a>.</p>

			<h2 id="end-connections" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">4. Grooved, Flanged and Threaded Ends</h2>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">The valve body is only half the specification; the ends decide how it goes into the pipe. Grooved ends are the fire protection default for above-ground mains because the coupling is a mechanical joint: no hot work, no flange bolt torque sequence, and the valve can be swapped out by loosening two couplings. That last point matters on a live system, where a flanged valve replacement means cutting pipe and re-drilling.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Flanged ends remain the norm on larger sizes, on pump connections and wherever the pipe itself is flanged or the specification is a water-standard rather than a fire listing. Drilling standard must be stated: a DN150 valve drilled to one standard will not bolt to a flange drilled to another, and this is one of the most common causes of a stalled installation. Our <a href="/products/resilient-seat-flanged-gate-valve-z41x/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">Z41X flanged resilient-seat gate valve</a> runs DN50 to DN700 (2 in to 28 in) at PN10/PN16 with an EPDM-encapsulated wedge and a flat-bottom body that resists sediment build-up at the seat.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Threaded ends are limited to small sizes and to trim, drains and gauges. They are quick and cheap but the thread is the weak point in both pressure rating and corrosion resistance, which is why fire mains above DN50 are rarely threaded.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Material selection follows the water, not the price list. On a treated or mildly aggressive supply a ductile iron body with an epoxy coating is adequate. Where chlorides or process water are involved, a stainless body is the cheaper answer over the life of the system: the <a href="/products/z85x-stainless-steel-grooved-gate-valve/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">Z85X grooved gate valve</a> in cast 316 stainless (CF8M) covers DN40-DN300 (2 in to 12 in) for water, gas and oil service. Note that this model is a non-rising stem design — if the installation needs visible indication, specify an OS&amp;Y valve instead.</p>

			<h2 id="installation-maintenance" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">5. Installation, Orientation and Maintenance</h2>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Orientation first. The correct installed positions are stem vertical (handwheel up) or stem horizontal. Never bonnet-down: the bonnet cavity becomes a trap for sediment and scale, and the packing gland ends up underneath the body where nobody will service it. On a rising-stem valve, check the full stem travel against the available space, including the handwheel swing, and confirm that nobody plans to hang a cable tray in that gap.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Support the valve; do not let the pipe carry it. A large flanged gate valve is a heavy item and its own weight plus thermal movement will load the joints. On grooved installations the couplings need the pipe ends cut square and free of burrs, with the correct gap and the housing bolts tightened to the coupling manufacturer's torque — an over-tightened coupling pinches the gasket and an under-tightened one leaks at pressure.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Operate it fully. Gate valves are isolation devices, not throttles: a part-open gate erodes its own seat and disc edge in the high-velocity jet, and on a fire main it is a silent loss of design flow. After any maintenance the valve goes back to fully open and is re-supervised. Exercise each control valve at least annually through a full open-close cycle — the most common failure mode of a valve that has sat open for five years is that it will not close when someone finally needs it to.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Maintenance itself is limited to the gland and the operator. Slight weeping at the packing is corrected by evenly tightening the gland nuts a fraction at a time; over-tightening scores the stem and makes the valve hard to operate. A stem that becomes stiff on an OS&amp;Y valve is usually a dry or dirty exposed thread, which is easy to clean and lubricate, and that accessibility is one of the design's practical advantages. Grooved installation practice is covered in more depth in our <a href="/guides/grooved-fire-protection-valves-guide/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">grooved fire protection valves guide</a>.</p>

			<h2 id="specification-checklist" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">6. Specification Checklist</h2>
			<ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
				<li><strong>Type and function.</strong> OS&amp;Y rising stem for above-ground control valves that require visible indication; NRS for buried service behind a post indicator or valve box.</li>
				<li><strong>Listing.</strong> State UL 262 or FM 1120/1130 listing, or the local equivalent, and require the certificate number for the model supplied. Listing applies to the valve, so the couplings must be listed or approved too.</li>
				<li><strong>Size and pressure class.</strong> Nominal size and rating — PN16 for most building fire mains, higher where the pump churn pressure requires it. Do not accept a rating that only covers static pressure.</li>
				<li><strong>End connection and drilling standard.</strong> Grooved to the coupling standard used on site, or flanged with the exact drilling standard named.</li>
				<li><strong>Trim and materials.</strong> Body material, wedge or disc seal, stem material and coating. State the water chemistry if it is aggressive.</li>
				<li><strong>Supervision.</strong> Tamper switch on every control valve, with voltage, contact type and the panel it reports to.</li>
				<li><strong>Operator details.</strong> Handwheel size, chainwheel where the valve is above reach, and the required clearance for stem travel.</li>
				<li><strong>Documentation.</strong> Material certificates, shell and seat test records, and the maintenance data — the same package as any fire valve order, summarised in the <a href="/guides/b2b-valve-sourcing-checklist/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">B2B valve sourcing checklist</a>.</li>
			</ul>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">For the wider system context — alarm valves, flow switches and test connections that sit alongside the riser valve — see the <a href="/guides/fire-sprinkler-system-valves-guide/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">fire sprinkler system valves guide</a>.</p>

			<h2 id="faq" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-6 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
			<div class="space-y-4">
				{faq.map(item => (
					<details class="group bg-gray-50 rounded-xl border border-gray-100 p-5 open:border-brand-red/30">
						<summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-gray-800 cursor-pointer list-none">
							<span>{item.question}</span>
							<span class="text-brand-blue text-xl transition-transform group-open:rotate-45">+</span>
						</summary>
						<p class="mt-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed text-sm">{item.answer}</p>
					</details>
				))}
			</div>

			<div class="mt-12 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
				<div class="bg-gradient-to-r from-brand-blue to-blue-800 px-6 sm:px-10 py-10 text-center">
					<h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white tracking-tight">Specifying OS&amp;Y Gate Valves?</h2>
					<p class="mt-3 text-blue-100 max-w-xl mx-auto text-sm leading-relaxed">Send the size schedule, pressure class, end type and the listing your project requires — our engineers confirm the model, trim and switch options and quote within 12 hours.</p>
					<div class="mt-6 flex flex-col sm:flex-row justify-center gap-4">
						<a href="/contact/?product=OS%26Y%20Gate%20Valve" class="px-8 py-4 bg-brand-red text-white font-bold rounded-lg hover:opacity-90 transition">Request a Quote</a>
						<a href="https://wa.me/8618684324501?text=Hi%2C%20I%20need%20OS%26Y%20gate%20valves%20for%20a%20fire%20main" class="px-8 py-4 bg-white text-brand-blue font-bold rounded-lg hover:bg-blue-50 transition" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Chat on WhatsApp</a>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</article>
	</main>
</Layout>
